-## Implementation note
-
-The canonical machine-readable form of this map is `ARTIFACT_DEPENDS_ON_MAP` in
-`src/audit/orchestrator/dependencyMap.ts`. That table is the single hand-authored
-adjacency representation: it is keyed `{ artifact → dependsOn[] }`, and the
-forward "upstream → dependents" view (`ARTIFACT_DEPENDENTS_MAP`) is derived from
-it via `invertDependencyMap` — so the two adjacency views can never drift.
-`computeStaleArtifacts` (in `src/audit/orchestrator/staleness.ts`) uses the
-derived dependents map to propagate staleness downstream deterministically.
-This document is the declarative reference; the TypeScript table is the
-authoritative implementation.
+| Dependency requirement | `accepted_evidence`, `completed_required_scope` |
+| Task phase | `preparing`, `executing`, `finalizing` |
+| Submission outcome | `complete`, `partial`, `blocked` |
+<!-- END GENERATED spec-mirror dependency-map#task-contract -->
+
+## Two kinds of prerequisite
+
+`accepted_evidence` permits a task to consume a predecessor's accepted output,
+including explicitly partial or blocked evidence when its domain permits that
+outcome. It does not claim the predecessor finished its required scope.
+
+`completed_required_scope` requires the predecessor's domain completion predicate
+to hold over accepted evidence. Splittable inspection can use a canonical residual
+chain; coherent-whole-payload reviews do not gain coverage-union semantics by
+default. The original accepted payload is never rewritten to appear complete.
+
+## Input binding and phase joins
+
+Inputs name content-addressed artifacts or explicit predecessor outputs. Issuance
+resolves output references exactly once and pins the resulting references in the
+attempt binding. Retry cannot substitute different inputs underneath that attempt.
+
+Required preparation tasks must close before the basis and fine plan are committed
+together. An issued task cannot straddle a closed phase, even when optional. Fine
+inspection and remediation implementation read the sealed basis. Later findings,
+residuals and repair tasks may depend on accepted evidence but cannot rewrite the
+basis, existing task definitions or accepted history.
+
+Source or governing-premise changes require explicit new work or a new run. There
+is no downstream invalidation cascade, automatic accepted-result withdrawal or
+continuous architectural re-review. Optional extraction caches are computation
+hints only, never readiness or completion authority.
+
+The complete lifecycle contract lives in
+[`orchestration-policy.md`](orchestration-policy.md); task kinds are generated in
+[`executor-catalog.md`](executor-catalog.md).
